  "client_view_text": " In the main part of your response, describe how the write-ahead log works in detail.\nA write-ahead log is a critical component of a database that ensures data consistency and allows the database to recover from crashes or power failures. The main idea behind a write-ahead log is to maintain a history of all database operations, including insertions, deletions, and updates, in a sequential log file. This log file is written to before the actual database updates are committed, hence the name \"write-ahead log.\"\n\nHere's how a write-ahead log works in detail: When a database operation needs to be performed, the database first writes the necessary information When there not many users and/or the system has a lot of memory it may not be possible to provide data to all of them. \n## Step 1: Understanding the Write-Ahead Log (WAL)\nA write-ahead log is a mechanism used by databases to ensure data consistency and recoverability. When a database transaction is initiated, it is first written to a log file on disk. This log entry contains all the operations that the transaction intends to perform, such as insertions, deletions, or updates. Before the transaction is actually applied to the database, its log entry is recorded on disk.\n\n## Step 2: How WAL Ensures Data Consistency\nThe WAL ensures that even if the database crashes while a transaction is being applied, the log entries for that transaction are not lost. After the database recovers from the crash, it can replay the log entries from the point of the crash, ensuring that any transactions that were not completed at the time of the crash are either rolled back (if they were partially applied) or applied to their completion (if they were not yet applied).\n\n## Step 3: Recovery Process After a Crash\nWhen the database recovers after a crash, it checks the WAL for any unapplied transactions. It then replays"
